# Security and Compliance Documentation

## Overview

This document outlines the comprehensive security strategy and compliance framework for the Amma Health platform. Given our target audience includes minors (students under 18), we prioritize COPPA compliance, data privacy, and robust security measures.

**Security Philosophy**: Security by design, privacy by default
**Compliance Framework**: COPPA, FERPA, GDPR (for future international expansion)
**Security Level**: Enterprise-grade security standards
**Last Updated**: October 8, 2025

## Compliance Requirements

### COPPA (Children's Online Privacy Protection Act)

**Scope**: All users under 13 years of age
**Enforcement**: Federal Trade Commission (FTC)
**Penalties**: Up to $43,792 per violation

#### COPPA Requirements Implementation

**Parental Consent Mechanism**:
```typescript
// COPPA compliance workflow
class COPPACompliance {
  async verifyAge(age: number): Promise<boolean> {
    if (age < 13) {
      return await this.requireParentalConsent();
    }
    return true;
  }

  async requireParentalConsent(): Promise<boolean> {
    // Send verification email to parent
    // Collect parent contact information
    // Obtain verifiable parental consent
    // Maintain consent records
    return false; // Until consent verified
  }

  async getDataCollectionDisclaimer(): Promise<string> {
    return `
      Amma Health collects limited personal information from children under 13 
      solely for the purpose of providing educational services. We do not 
      sell or share this information with third parties. Parents can review, 
      modify, or delete their child's information at any time.
    `;
  }
}
```

**Data Collection Limitations**:
- Minimal data collection for users under 13
- No behavioral advertising for minors
- Limited data retention periods
- Parent access to child's data
- Easy data deletion requests

**Privacy Policy Requirements**:
- Clear, age-appropriate language
- Detailed data collection practices
- Parent rights and responsibilities
- Contact information for privacy inquiries

### FERPA (Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act)

**Scope**: Educational records for students of all ages
**Enforcement**: U.S. Department of Education
**Requirements**: Parental access to educational records

#### FERPA Compliance Implementation

**Educational Record Definition**:
```typescript
interface EducationalRecord {
  studentId: string;
  academicProgress: AcademicProgress;
  learningAnalytics: LearningAnalytics;
  teacherNotes: TeacherNote[];
  parent communications: ParentCommunication[];
  assessmentResults: AssessmentResult[];
}
```

**Parent Access Rights**:
- View all educational records
- Request corrections to inaccurate records
- Control disclosure of records
- File complaints with FERPA office

**School Partnerships**:
- Data sharing agreements with schools
- Secure data transmission protocols
- Compliance with school district policies
- Audit trails for data access

### GDPR (General Data Protection Regulation)

**Scope**: Future international expansion (EU users)
**Enforcement**: EU Data Protection Authorities
**Penalties**: Up to €20 million or 4% of global revenue

#### GDPR Compliance Preparation

**Key Principles**:
- Lawfulness, fairness, and transparency
- Purpose limitation
- Data minimization
- Accuracy
- Storage limitation
- Integrity and confidentiality
- Accountability

**User Rights Implementation**:
```typescript
class GDPRCompliance {
  async dataPortability(userId: string): Promise<DataExport> {
    // Export all user data in machine-readable format
    return await this.exportUserData(userId);
  }

  async rightToErasure(userId: string): Promise<boolean> {
    // Delete user data and all associated records
    await this.deleteUserData(userId);
    await this.deleteFromBackups(userId);
    return true;
  }

  async rectificationRequest(userId: string, corrections: DataCorrections): Promise<boolean> {
    // Update inaccurate user data
    await this.updateUserData(userId, corrections);
    return true;
  }
}
```

## Security Architecture

### Defense in Depth Strategy

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "Security Layers"
        A[Network Security] --> B[Application Security]
        B --> C[Data Security]
        C --> D[Infrastructure Security]
    end

    subgraph "Security Controls"
        E[Firewalls & WAF] --> F[Authentication & Authorization]
        F --> G[Encryption & Key Management]
